			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-520649">
									

<p>Assuming, of course, that the person knows how to erase records <em>completely</em> in the first place. It's harder then you might think, especially on large systems where the contents of a file might be spread and duplicated among several different, secure subsystems. Now, if a god wanted to delete a file completely, that'd be another thing. But if a god just let someone else do what they wanted and tweaked things so they wouldn't be noticed? Seems fine to me.</p>
<p>All I'm saying is that the old version could most easily be interpreted in a completely benign light while at the same time not ruling out a more sinister interpretation for those who really wanted to see one - it seemed to be very well balanced and not in any need of major revisions to me. Plus, even if far2 wasn't the original author, <em>neither are you</em>, and while I do see some complaints here, I <em>don't</em> see any exchanges along the lines of, "Hey, I'm not the original author but I want to change this to [X], can I?" "Yeah, sure, go ahead."</p>

							</div>

								
